<node id="model:huburns_updated_pour_blend">
  <data key="d0">equation</data>
  <data key="d1">Hu-Burns power-law pour point blending, updated exponents by range (Semwal and Varshney)</data>
  <data key="d2">correlations</data>
  <data key="d3">pour_point</data>
  <data key="d4">secondary_citation</data>
  <data key="d5">medium</data>
  <data key="d6">def pp_blend(V, PP, x=0.079):
    import numpy as np
    R = (np.asarray(PP, float) + 273.15)*1.8
    return (np.sum(np.asarray(V, float)*R**(1.0/x)))**x/1.8 - 273.15</data>
  <data key="d7">{"note": "fuel oil and distillate blends; choose x by blend pour point range"}</data>
</node>
<node id="url:https://www.ogj.com/home/article/17211400/new-pour-point-correlations-improve-economics-of-distillate-blending">
  <data key="d0">source</data>
  <data key="d1">Semwal, P.B., Varshney, R.G. (1994). New pour point correlations improve economics of distillate blending. Oil and Gas Journal, June 6, 1994 (updated x values);</data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
  <data key="d9">https://www.ogj.com/home/article/17211400/new-pour-point-correlations-improve-economics-of-distillate-blending</data>
</node>
